From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 02:58:41 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: lantiq: do not learn addresses from the xRX500 processor
 port

Every frame the software forwarding path sends enters the switch on the
processor port carrying the sending station's own address. A switch that
learns from that port therefore records every station against the
processor, and no entry in the address table ever names the port a
station is really on - so the fabric can never forward a frame between
two front ports, and every one of them crosses the processor instead.

Turn learning off on the processor port and leave it on for the ports
that face a wire, which is what makes an entry name the real port. The
vendor switch library writes the same field from its per-port
configuration entry point, and its xRX500 ethernet driver turns learning
off across a whole switch macro at bring-up.

The two neighbouring fields of the port's other parser control word are
deliberately left alone. Port locking and aging disable are written by
that same vendor function, but only ever from values its caller passes
in, and no in-kernel caller of it sets either one: they belong to the
address-learning limit and spoofing-detection feature, which is driven
from user space and carries no datapath requirement. Setting them here
would cap the table and stop it ageing for reasons this driver does not
have.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
index 0fd8bab440bf..aefd916f9c38 100644
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
@@ -710,6 +710,7 @@ static int gswip_xrx500_setup(struct dsa_switch *ds)
 {
 	struct gswip_priv *priv = ds->priv;
 	const struct gswip_xrx500_model *model = gswip_xrx500_model(priv);
+	struct dsa_port *cpu_dp;
 	int err;
 
 	/* The queue manager's drop-policy selector is two bits wide here, and
@@ -725,6 +726,24 @@ static int gswip_xrx500_setup(struct dsa_switch *ds)
 	 * whenever it forwards for the switch.
 	 */
 
+	/* Every frame the software forwarding path sends arrives at the switch
+	 * on the processor port carrying the sending station's address, so a
+	 * switch that learns from the processor port ends up with every
+	 * station recorded against it. From then on the fabric has an address
+	 * table entry for each station and it names the processor, so a frame
+	 * between two front ports is delivered to the processor instead of
+	 * being forwarded in hardware - which is worse than having no entry at
+	 * all, because an unknown destination is flooded to every port.
+	 *
+	 * Learning on the processor port therefore has to be off. The ports
+	 * that face a wire keep it, which is what makes an entry name the port
+	 * a station is really on.
+	 */
+	dsa_switch_for_each_cpu_port(cpu_dp, ds)
+		regmap_set_bits(priv->gswip,
+				GSWIP_PCE_PCTRL_3p(cpu_dp->index),
+				GSWIP_PCE_PCTRL_3_LNDIS);
+
 	/* Address the egress table by traffic class rather than by the
 	 * processing flags of the accelerator paths, which this design does
 	 * not build. This has to be settled before the table is written,
